#: views/documentation.php:186
msgid "Move managed packages without copying a development checkout"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:187
msgid "A Transporter Blueprint is a ZIP file that records the Booster-managed packages you select on this site and where their repos live. It is not a copy of the plugin or theme files installed here. The site that opens the Blueprint downloads its files from the committed repository, so local uncommitted changes, and local-only"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:187
msgid "folders, and other ignored and uncommitted dependencies are never included."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:188
msgid "Before a database or environment move"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:190
msgid "Export a current Transporter Blueprint containing every managed package."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:191
msgid "Optionally include the selected packages’ file-stored repository credentials in the password-protected archive."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:192
msgid "Preview the ZIP successfully and retain a copy off-site."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:193
msgid "Keep the normal database and filesystem backup."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:195
msgid "Complete database copies inside the supported MySQL or MariaDB and InnoDB envelope are best effort. A Transporter Blueprint is the supported reconstruction route when the copied Booster tables cannot be trusted, but the target database must still satisfy Booster’s requirements. Booster does not convert custom tables between database engines."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:196
msgid "Deployment attempts and delivery-replay history, webhook secrets and provider-side hooks, constants, locks and worker state, and source deployment policy remain target-local. Every installed or adopted package starts with deployment Disabled, so an administrator must deliberately choose a new target policy."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:197
msgid "Transporter workflow:"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:197
msgid "Select packages for a Transporter Blueprint on this site, open it on the other site, review what would change for each package, then apply only the selected changes. Excluded and unchecked packages remain untouched. Reviewing a Transporter Blueprint never installs anything, changes package settings, or stores the uploaded file — nothing happens until you apply it."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:198
msgid "Open Transporter"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:200
msgid "Step-by-step: moving a package to another site"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:202
msgid "Commit and push the exact branch, tag or commit you want the other site to install. A Transporter Blueprint never includes uncommitted local changes."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:203
msgid "If you are actively editing a package’s files on this site, set it to Disabled first so the Transporter Blueprint does not propose replacing files you are working on. Use Manual only when you want administrators on the other site to be able to update it through Booster."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:204
msgid "Choose which managed plugins and themes to include, then create a Transporter Blueprint. Credential transfer applies only to those selected packages and only when their credential is stored as a file rather than, for example, a constant. A package-only ZIP is unencrypted; including credentials requires a password and AES-256 encryption."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:205
msgid "On the other site, upload the ZIP, enter its password if you set one, then select Review Transporter Blueprint. Booster shows which packages it can install or adopt and which are already managed, protected, or blocked. An already-managed row can import its verified Blueprint credential without changing package settings; select only the actions you want Booster to apply."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:206
msgid "Apply only the changes you choose. Booster re-reads the ZIP and re-checks each package and repository right before applying it, so if something changed since your review, that one change is skipped safely and the others continue."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:207
msgid "On the other site, confirm repository access works and the package behaves as expected. Set up Push-to-Deploy separately, and only once that site is ready to receive automatic deployments."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:215
msgid "Move retained WP Pusher packages into Booster"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:216
msgid "If this site used WP Pusher 3.0.13, the temporary RAN Booster WP Pusher Migrator can adopt supported installed plugins and themes into Booster without reinstalling them. Each adopted package starts with deployment Disabled."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:218
msgid "Before you start"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:220
msgid "Back up the site and deactivate WP Pusher. Booster and WP Pusher must not control package changes at the same time."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:221
msgid "Install and activate the migrator from its verified release package."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:222
msgid "For private repositories, add the replacement repository credential to Booster first. WP Pusher credentials are not copied."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:225
msgid "Migration workflow"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:227
msgid "Open Transporter and choose Migrate from WP Pusher."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:228
msgid "Review one retained plugin or theme at a time."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:229
msgid "Adopt the package into Booster as Disabled."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:230
msgid "Confirm the package is managed by Booster before continuing."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:231
msgid "After every package is migrated, review the optional WP Pusher data cleanup and remove the temporary migrator."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:233
msgid "The migrator does not copy credentials, reinstall package files, enable deployments, remove remote webhooks, delete WP Pusher, or contact its license service. Remove provider-side deployment webhooks separately."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:234
msgid "Open migration tools"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:241
msgid "Deployment modes protect files you are editing"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:243
msgid "blocks any replacement, whether triggered by an administrator or by a webhook. Use this for a package whose files on disk you are currently editing."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:244
msgid "blocks webhook-triggered replacement (Push-to-Deploy) but still lets an administrator replace the package’s files through Booster."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:245
msgid "allows a matching, signed push from the repository provider to replace the package automatically. Only turn this on for a site that is ready to receive live deployments."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:247
msgid "Booster never changes a package’s mode on its own. It may show a reminder when WordPress reports a local or development environment, plugin or theme development mode is active, WP_DEBUG is enabled, the site uses localhost or a loopback address, or its URL has a nonstandard port. These are safety hints only: Booster will not switch a package to Disabled or Manual for you. Choose each package’s mode explicitly."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:249
msgid "What a Transporter Blueprint includes"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:250
msgid "A Transporter Blueprint records only the Booster-managed plugins and themes selected on the source site. It can optionally include file-stored repository credentials used by those selected packages inside a password-protected ZIP. The ZIP password is only used for that one request and is never saved anywhere. When a package is installed or adopted from a Transporter Blueprint, it starts with deployment Disabled."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:251
msgid "A Transporter Blueprint does not include WordPress content, uploads, the database, the package files themselves, constants, webhook secrets, provider-side hooks, deployment attempts or delivery-replay history, locks or worker state, or source deployment policy. Push-to-Deploy is always configured separately on each site."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:253
msgid "Prepare the branch you are deploying"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:254
msgid "Booster installs exactly what is committed to the repository ref you select. It does not remove files, run build tools or install dependencies on the target site."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:256
msgid "You, or the package’s maintainer, are responsible for what the repository’s ignore, build and release process leaves in that ref."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:257
msgid "Include compiled or built assets and anything the package needs at runtime."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:258
msgid "is normally only needed to build the package, so it should usually be excluded from the ref."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:259
msgid "Include the Composer"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:259
msgid "directory if the package needs it at runtime and does not install it itself."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:267
msgid "Where credentials are stored"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:268
msgid "Booster stores repository credentials and webhook secrets as authenticated ciphertext in a private JSON file outside the WordPress and plugin directories. Conventional single-site POSIX installations can use the protected Overview’s automatic location. Containers and uncommon layouts should define"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:268
msgid "as a private directory outside the public web root on durable local storage. The configured value must evaluate to an absolute canonical path; in wp-config.php, an expression such as"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:268
msgid "provides a safe relative layout anchored to that file. The directory must be owned by PHP, readable and writable by PHP, and mode 0700; Booster manages secrets.json inside it. Existing configurations may continue using the exact-file"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:268
msgid "with the same dirname( __DIR__ ) anchoring. Raw unanchored relative strings are rejected because web, cron and CLI processes may have different working directories. Booster never displays a saved secret back to you once it is stored."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:269
msgid "The file and lock are owner-only. Its independent encryption key is held in the non-autoloaded"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:269
msgid "WordPress option, so neither the filesystem nor database alone contains usable credentials. Restore the matching encrypted file and database key from the same backup, and protect that backup as secret material."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:271
#: views/onboarding.php:232
msgid "Use a different storage location"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:272
msgid "Choose a durable directory outside the public web root that is owned by the same PHP process user used by WordPress web, cron and CLI requests. The directory must use mode 0700. Do not add group access as a workaround: Booster deliberately requires owner-only access. Define the preferred directory constant in wp-config.php before WordPress loads plugins; Booster creates and manages secrets.json and secrets.json.lock inside it with mode 0600:"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:274
msgid "In wp-config.php, __DIR__ is the directory containing wp-config.php and dirname( __DIR__ ) is its parent. If wp-config.php is in public_html, do not append public_html again: choose a sibling directory outside it. Every existing parent directory must let the PHP process traverse it; on shared hosting that may require a host-managed execute permission or ACL on an ancestor. The final Booster storage directory must still be owned by PHP and mode 0700."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:275
msgid "To use a hosting environment variable, bridge it to the PHP constant in wp-config.php. An environment variable by itself is not read by Booster:"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:280
msgid "The legacy exact-file constant remains supported and can use the same wp-config.php-relative anchor:"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:282
msgid "If credentials already exist, move or restore secrets.json, secrets.json.lock and the matching database key together. Do not create an empty secrets file, copy only the encrypted file, or reset only the database key. If either the file or database key is permanently unavailable, the protected Overview can offer an explicit typed reset after Booster verifies the remaining filesystem material and private path; this discards unrecoverable credentials and webhook secrets before fresh storage can be initialized."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:283
msgid "Moving credentials between sites"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:284
msgid "A plugin ZIP export or a database-only site migration cannot recreate the encrypted store. Transporter, described above, is the supported way to move selected repository credentials: it protects them inside the Transporter Blueprint ZIP, confirms repository identity and access on the target, and re-encrypts them with the target site’s key. Installing or adopting a package associates the imported credential with that package. Credential-only recovery for an already-managed package imports the credential under a new target-local ID but deliberately leaves the package’s saved credential selection unchanged; assign the recovered credential through the package settings if needed. Constants, webhook secrets and credentials already saved on the target are never exported. When Transporter is not the right fit, add credentials directly through the provider settings screens on the target site, or use the supported deployment constants instead."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:285
msgid "Do not overwrite an existing target site’s credentials file. If you ever need to restore the whole file as a recovery step, keep a copy of the target’s existing file first, and treat that restore as something only an approved recovery process should do."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:286
msgid "Keep the encrypted file and key protected, and exclude the file from version control and from any release archive."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:288
msgid "What happens on uninstall"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:289
msgid "Deactivating Booster keeps all credentials, package settings and deployment history in place. Deleting Booster through WordPress is the permanent local cleanup action: it removes both Booster custom tables, the encrypted credentials file and its separate database key, other Booster options, user notices, scheduled work, locks and temporary Logging capture. If Booster cannot verify or remove one of those artifacts safely, WordPress keeps the plugin files so an administrator can correct the problem and retry."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:290
msgid "Before deleting Booster, export a password-protected Transporter Blueprint with the selected packages and any file-stored repository credentials they use that you may need. A Transporter Blueprint does not include webhook secrets, provider-side webhook registrations, deployment history or constants, and every restored package starts with deployment Disabled."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:291
msgid "Uninstall cannot revoke provider credentials or remove remote webhooks. Revoke credentials with each provider and remove remote hooks before uninstalling. Booster removes only the exact wp-config.php definition it created; a manually authored"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:291
msgid "definition remains under the site operator’s control."
msgstr ""

